# Claimed Write Scope

Use this machine-readable section inside `PLAN::ISSUE-ID` for any task that may run through `/orchestrator`.

```markdown
## Claimed Write Scope

```json
{
  "areas": ["billing", "api"],
  "paths": [
    "src/modules/billing/service.ts",
    "src/modules/billing/service.test.ts"
  ],
  "shared_surfaces": [
    "src/contracts/billing.ts"
  ],
  "related_issues": ["PROJ-456"],
  "touches_process_layer": false
}
```
```

## Field rules

- `areas`: logical business areas/modules touched by the issue.
- `paths`: exact file paths that will be created or modified.
- `shared_surfaces`: exact high-conflict files that multiple areas depend on.
- `related_issues`: issue IDs that must not execute in parallel even without path overlap.
- `touches_process_layer`: `true` when changing workflow/process surfaces like `.ai/*`, `.cursor/*`, `.codex/*`, `.agent/*`, `.agents/*`, `ai.config.yaml`, `AGENTS.md`, `MANIFESTO.md`, or `Taskfile.yml`.

## Constraints

- `paths` and `shared_surfaces` must be file-level claims, not directories.
- If a future file does not exist yet, use its exact intended path from the plan.
- If `touches_process_layer` is `true`, at least one claimed file must live in the process layer.
- If deterministic file-level scope is impossible, the plan is not orchestrator-ready.
